    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.completion\n\n### Description\n\nmini.completion works great as a light completion plugin with sane defaults. However I find it has a major annoyance: once the completion menu shows up, pressing `<CR>` (i.e. Enter) when no item is selected from the completion menu causes the menu to disappear, but *no newline character is added to the buffer*. (To reliably reproduce, this requires some delay between the keystrokes, a fraction of a second is enough.)\r\n\r\nThis is bad because, given a sequence of keystrokes, what actually goes into the buffer depends on whether the completion menu appeared during the sequence, and on how many times it happened. In other words, given a sequence of keystrokes, what goes into the buffer is not deterministic.\r\n\r\nI know you have a workaround for this (https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.completion/blob/6eb954668/doc/mini-completion.txt#L129), but I think this should be the default behavior. Or there should be an easy-to-set config option.\r\n\r\n(Am I missing something? How are other people dealing with this?)\r\n\r\nThanks!\r\n\r\n",
