        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> I just have one follow-up question: don't you think that the fact that `<CR>` sometimes dismisses the menu with an actual `<CR>`, and sometimes it does not, is a bug _somewhere_, maybe in neovim itself?\r\n\r\nThis is just how built-in Insert completion works with 'completopt' set to `menuone,noselect`. You can replicate it without 'mini.completion':\r\n\r\n- `nvim --clean`\r\n- `:set completeopt=menuone,noselect`\r\n- Type `foobar`.\r\n- Type `fo` on the new line. Press `<C-n>` (popup appears) and then `<CR>` (popup disappears and new line is entered).\r\n- Type `f` on the new line. Press `<C-n>`. Type `o` (popup is updated) and then `<CR>` (popup disappears and no new line is added).\r\n\r\nI believe this is a documented behaivor, see `:h popupmenu-keys` (scroll down for `<Enter>` description) and `:h ins-completion-menu` (for description of three states). It does indeed seem a bit off that typing `o` without actually selecting any entry puts completion in second state, but I am quite sure that this is somehow an intended behavior in Vim (not even Neovim).",
            "created_at": "2025-01-07T18:46:15Z",
            "html_url": "https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/issues/1469#issuecomment-2575996290",
